6. FOLDER TOUR (WHAT EACH FILE IS FOR)
================================================================

vuln_aggregator/
|
|-- .env / .env.example
|     Your secrets and settings. .env.example is a template you can copy.
|     Never publish a real .env (passwords live there). .gitignore already
|     tells git to ignore .env.
|
|-- docker-compose.yml
|     The "start the whole factory" file. It boots:
|       postgres   = the database
|       redis      = optional cache (configured, not heavily used yet)
|       api        = FastAPI web service
|       scheduler  = automatic repeating scans
|       dashboard  = Streamlit website
|
|-- Dockerfile
|     Recipe for building the API / scheduler Python image.
|
|-- requirements.txt
|     List of Python libraries the backend needs (FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, ...).
|
|-- README.md
|     Short official overview and how to run Docker.
|
|-- noob.read
|     This file. The long beginner explanation.
|
|-- src/                         THE BRAIN
|   |-- config.py                Reads .env into a Settings object
|   |-- models.py                The common "Vulnerability" form + ID helper
|   |-- database.py              PostgreSQL table definition and connection
|   |-- orchestrator.py          The project manager (scan, enrich, store)
|   |-- risk_engine.py           The 0-10 scoring formula
|   |-- scheduler.py             Alarm clock: scan every N hours
|   |
|   |-- connectors/              TRANSLATORS TO OUTSIDE TOOLS
|   |     base.py                The contract every scanner must follow
|   |     gvm_connector.py       Talks to Greenbone / OpenVAS
|   |     nessus_connector.py    Talks to Nessus
|   |     vulners_connector.py   Talks to Vulners threat intel
|   |
|   |-- api/                     THE DOOR TO THE OUTSIDE WORLD
|   |     main.py                Starts FastAPI, creates DB tables on boot
|   |     routes.py              The actual URLs: /scans and /findings
|   |
|   |-- utils/
|         logging.py             Structured JSON logs
|
|-- dashboard/
|     app.py                     Streamlit UI (metrics, charts, button)
|     Dockerfile / requirements.txt
|
|-- tests/
      Empty package for future tests.


================================================================
7. THE COMMON FORM: WHAT A "VULNERABILITY" RECORD HOLDS
================================================================

Every finding, no matter which scanner found it, is squeezed into this
shape (src/models.py). That is the whole point of "unified".

  id                 Fingerprint so the same issue stays one row
  cve_id             Public catalog number, if known
  scanner            "gvm" or "nessus"
  scanner_vuln_id    That scanner's own ID for the check (plugin / NVT)
  asset_host         Hostname you asked to scan
  asset_ip           IP where it was found
  asset_os           Operating system, if known
  port / protocol    Which door, and TCP or UDP
  description        Human-readable "what is wrong"
  cvss_v2_score
  cvss_v3_score      How bad it is on the 0-10 CVSS scale
  severity           Low / Medium / High / Critical
  solution           How to fix it (patch, config change, ...)
  exploit_available  True if public exploit intel exists
  epss_score         Likelihood of real-world exploitation
  references         Links
  risk_score         THIS app's 0-10 priority number
  scan_timestamp     When we last saw it
  tags               Extra labels (mostly unused for now)

THE STABLE ID
  generate_vuln_id() hashes:
      scanner + scanner_vuln_id + asset_ip + port
  Same inputs always produce the same id. That is how upserts work.

  Example meaning: "Nessus plugin 12345 on 192.168.1.10 port 443" is always
  the same finding, even if you scan again tomorrow.


================================================================
8. CONNECTORS: WHY THEY EXIST
================================================================

GVM and Nessus are different products.

  - GVM speaks GMP and returns XML.
  - Nessus speaks HTTP JSON (via the nessrest library).
  - Vulners is not a scanner; it is a lookup service for CVE intel.

If the rest of the app had to know XML vs JSON, the orchestrator would
become a mess. So each connector hides the ugly details.

base.py says: "If you want to be a scanner here, you MUST implement
scan_target() and fetch_results() and return a list of Vulnerability."

That is an interface / contract. Later you could add Qualys or Rapid7 by
writing one new connector file, without rewriting the dashboard.

GVM POLLING
  After you start a GVM task, it does not finish instantly. The code sleeps
  10 seconds, asks "are you done?", repeats, until status is no longer
  Running / Requested / Queued. If that takes more than 2 hours, it raises
  a timeout error instead of looping forever.

NESSUS POLLING
  Same idea, but it checks every 30 seconds, up to 1 hour.


================================================================
9. RISK SCORING, WITH A TINY EXAMPLE
================================================================

Formula (simplified):

  cvss_part     = CVSS * 0.5          (uses v3 if present, else v2)
  epss_part     = EPSS * 10 * 0.3     (EPSS 0.2 contributes 0.6)
  exploit_bonus = 1.5 if exploit else 0
  score         = min(cvss_part + epss_part + exploit_bonus, 10)
  final         = min(score * asset_criticality, 10)

Example finding:
  CVSS v3 = 8.0
  EPSS    = 0.5   (50% chance of exploitation, quite high)
  exploit exists
  asset_criticality = 1.0

  cvss_part     = 8.0 * 0.5 = 4.0
  epss_part     = 0.5 * 10 * 0.3 = 1.5
  exploit_bonus = 1.5
  total         = 7.0

That 7.0 is more useful than "High" because it mixes "how bad" with
"how likely attackers will actually use this".

If the scanner gave no CVSS, the code maps:
  Low=2.0, Medium=5.0, High=7.5, Critical=9.5
and then still applies the 50% weight.


================================================================
10. THE API AND THE DASHBOARD
================================================================

API (src/api/)
  FastAPI is a Python framework that turns functions into HTTP endpoints.

  POST /scans
    Body: a list of targets, e.g. ["192.168.1.0/24"]
    It does NOT wait for the scan to finish (scans can take a long time).
    It starts the work in the background and immediately replies
    { "status": "started", "targets": [...] }.

  GET /findings?severity=High&limit=100
    Reads the database. Optional filter by severity. Sorted worst-first.

  GET /findings/{id}
    One finding, or HTTP 404 if that id does not exist.

  On startup, main.py runs init_db() so the vulnerabilities table exists.

  Open http://localhost:8000/docs for a clickable manual (Swagger UI).

DASHBOARD (dashboard/app.py)
  Streamlit is a Python library that turns a script into a simple website.
  It calls the API (http://api:8000 when running inside Docker).

  You see:
    - total findings, count of Critical, count of High, average risk
    - top 10 by risk_score
    - a bar chart of severity
    - a line chart of findings per day
    - a button that POSTs a scan of 192.168.1.0/24

  Results are cached for 300 seconds so the page does not hammer the API
  on every refresh.


================================================================
11. THE SCHEDULER (THE ALARM CLOCK)
================================================================

src/scheduler.py uses APScheduler.

When the scheduler container starts, it:
  1. Runs a scan immediately
  2. Repeats every SCAN_SCHEDULE_HOURS hours

It reads SCAN_TARGETS from .env, then calls the same Orchestrator.scan_targets
that the API uses. So "click the button" and "wait for the clock" share one
brain.

docker-compose waits until Postgres is healthy before starting the
scheduler, so it does not try to write findings into a database that is
still booting.


================================================================
12. DOCKER COMPOSE: THE FIVE BOXES
================================================================

When you run:

  docker-compose up --build

Docker starts five services:

  postgres     Stores findings. Data lives in a volume named postgres_data
               so it survives container restarts.
  redis        A fast in-memory store. Wired in config as REDIS_URL.
  api          The FastAPI app on port 8000 (your PC: http://localhost:8000).
  scheduler    Background worker that triggers scans on a timer.
  dashboard    Streamlit on port 8501 (http://localhost:8501).

"Port 8000:8000" means: the program inside the box listens on 8000, and
your Windows browser can reach it at localhost:8000.

The api service mounts ./src into the container so code changes can reload
without a full rebuild (--reload).

You still need REAL scanner credentials in .env. Without a live GVM/Nessus
and a Vulners API key, scans will fail when they try to connect. The rest
of the stack (API + empty database + dashboard) can still start.


================================================================
13. THE .env FILE (WHAT YOU MUST FILL IN)
================================================================

  GVM_HOST / GVM_PORT / GVM_USERNAME / GVM_PASSWORD
      Where your Greenbone/OpenVAS server lives and how to log in.

  NESSUS_URL / NESSUS_ACCESS_KEY / NESSUS_SECRET_KEY
      Your Nessus (or Tenable) API access.

  VULNERS_API_KEY
      Key for exploit / EPSS lookups.

  POSTGRES_*
      Database login. The docker-compose defaults match the example:
      user vuln, password vulnpass, database vulndb.

  SCAN_TARGETS
      Comma-separated list of IPs, hostnames, or subnets to scan.

  SCAN_SCHEDULE_HOURS
      How often the scheduler repeats (default 6).

  API_HOST / API_PORT
      Where FastAPI listens (0.0.0.0 means "all interfaces", port 8000).


================================================================
14. HOW TO RUN IT (BEGINNER STEPS)
================================================================

  1. Install Docker Desktop on Windows.
  2. Open a terminal in the vuln_aggregator folder.
  3. Copy settings:
       copy .env.example .env     (or just edit .env; it already exists)
  4. Put real credentials in .env. Only scan networks you own or have
     written permission to test.
  5. Start everything:
       docker-compose up --build
  6. Wait until Postgres is healthy and the API starts.
  7. Browser:
       http://localhost:8000/docs     API playground
       http://localhost:8501          Dashboard
  8. Stop:
       Ctrl+C in the terminal, or:
       docker-compose down


================================================================
15. A STORY: ONE FINDING FROM START TO FINISH
================================================================

You scan 192.168.1.50.

Nessus reports: plugin 12345, CVE-2017-0144, port 445, severity High,
CVSS 8.1, description about an old Windows file-sharing bug.

GVM might report the same CVE on the same host, or a different issue.

The Nessus connector builds a Vulnerability object with scanner="nessus".
The ID is a hash of nessus + 12345 + 192.168.1.50 + 445.

Vulners is asked about CVE-2017-0144. It says exploits exist and EPSS is
high. exploit_available becomes True.

compute_risk produces something like 7+ out of 10.

Dedup checks whether this exact key already appeared in this batch.

PostgreSQL upserts that id. Tomorrow's scan updates scan_timestamp and
maybe the score, instead of creating "row 2, row 3, row 4".

The dashboard shows it near the top if the score is high. You patch the
machine (or disable the service), scan again later, and either the finding
disappears or its details get refreshed.


================================================================
16. WHAT THIS PROJECT IS *NOT*
================================================================

  - It is not an exploit tool. It collects and ranks defensive scan data.
  - It is not a replacement for GVM or Nessus. It sits ON TOP of them.
  - It does not (yet) log in users, send Slack alerts, or store how
    important each asset is. Those are listed as future extensions.
  - Redis is included in compose but the core flow does not depend on it
    for scoring or storage.
  - tests/ is a placeholder; there is no full automated test suite yet.


================================================================
17. MENTAL MAP (KEEP THIS)
================================================================

  You / Clock / Dashboard button
              |
              v
         Orchestrator
          /         \
       GVM          Nessus          <- scanners look at your network
          \         /
           findings (same format)
                |
             Vulners                <- extra intel about CVEs
                |
           Risk engine              <- 0-10 score
                |
           Dedup + Database
                |
        API  <---->  Dashboard      <- you look at the ranked list


If you remember only one sentence:

  This app runs two vulnerability scanners together, translates their
  reports into one language, adds exploit intel, scores each finding,
  stores it, and shows you what to fix first.

Welcome to the intersection of Python, networking, and defensive security.
You are not expected to understand every library on day one. Follow one
finding through orchestrator.py and you will understand the whole system.
